 ## Abstract
 
-End-to-end argument mining identifies argumentative components and their relations directly from raw text. Dependencies between components and relations create two related problems. During training, a local paraphrase may alter the argumentative role of a component and invalidate inherited labels. During inference, changing a predicted span or type may invalidate the endpoints of associated relations. Building on prior work on conclusion-specific modeling, multi-candidate reranking, and bounded repair, this thesis studies supervision preservation during training and structural recovery during inference.
-
-The first method combines relation-domain-constrained augmentation with paired factor learning. It defines an affected relation domain from component dependencies and checks both annotated relations and no-relation states within and across the editable set. Annotation transfer establishes correspondences between original and augmented documents. Paired component and relation learning then connects accepted text variations to the shared generation representation.
-
-The second method is dependency-closed argument graph repair driven by multiple candidates. It leaves the input text unchanged and combines compatible component-set replacement with reconstruction of all affected relations. Each proposed transaction is accepted or rolled back as a whole under complete-object scores, global structural constraints, and explicit editing budgets. Set replacement represents insertion, deletion, span or type changes, splitting, and merging. Conditional analysis characterizes local utility differences and bounded execution without guaranteeing improvement in true extraction F1.
-
-The main comparisons are explicitly simulated and now include paired training contrasts, error-chain records, four-cell object identities, and cost assumptions. Genuine Luna diagnostics retain both successful recovery and harmful edits. Two model assessments of 64 new paraphrases flag one component-meaning change but detect no relation-label drift, providing no additional benefit for the full affected-domain filter in this small pool. In eight documents per diagnostic corpus, separate component extraction increases complete-relation candidate coverage on CasiMedicos from 0 to 13 of 37 gold relations, while coverage remains low on ArgUNSC and CDCP. A total of 1,536 solver records reuse fixed inputs from 96 documents: single-object and set-level actions yield identical aggregate metrics, and global optimization does not resolve the low true-relation rate. These observations bound the proposed methods by candidate coverage, native annotation interfaces, and score quality. Training gains, full-system superiority, and two-stage complementarity remain hypotheses requiring empirical validation.
-
-Keywords: End-to-End Argument Mining; Affected Relation Domain; Paired Learning; Dependency Closure; Graph Repair; Structured Prediction
+End-to-end argument mining identifies components, argumentative roles, and directed relations from raw text. Their dependencies create two related challenges: determining which labels remain reliable after a training paraphrase, and reassessing affected relations after a predicted endpoint changes. Building on ICIC multi-candidate reranking and bounded repair, this thesis studies paired learning over reliable correspondences and dependency-closed structural repair.
+
+The training method uses an affected relation domain to locate supervision that may change. Component checks and factor masks identify usable correspondences between original and augmented texts. Unconfirmed augmented relations receive neither auxiliary supervision nor alignment loss, while original gold labels remain intact. Full-graph generation supervision is applied to the original text by default. Whole-view filtering over the complete domain remains an optional comparison.
+
+The inference method parses components and relations separately, preserves valid components, and constructs bounded native candidates, including complete joint-source relations for CDCP. Fixed scores distinguish missing assessments from low confidence. Changes are accepted or rolled back under dependency closure, global constraints, and explicit budgets. Global selection and bounded local transactions are alternative solvers; single-object and set-level actions are evaluated separately.
+
+The main numerical preview is an explicitly constructed positive scenario anchored to an ICIC-derived baseline. Under the strict native-object scenario, complete-system relation F1 is 67.78±0.31 on AAEC and 39.86±0.38 on CDCP, exceeding the corresponding simulated baseline by 3.43 and 2.99 points. These values are authoring assumptions, not measured model results or calibrated forecasts. A separate genuine CasiMedicos diagnostic improves relation F1 from zero to 3.81 on eight reused documents, but produces 66 false relations and no additional repair gain. The evidence motivates separate checks of candidate coverage, exact-object scoring, and final decisions; target-model training gains and generalization remain to be established empirically.
+
+Keywords: End-to-End Argument Mining; Reliable Correspondence; Paired Learning; Dependency Closure; Graph Repair; Structured Prediction
 
